  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nA band of very light precipitation continues to cross the Northland\nfrom west to east through the rest of this afternoon and tonight.\nBecause of very dry air in the lowest few thousand feet and minimal\nlarge-scale forcing associated with this very weak warm front, \nmuch of this moisture is evaporating before reaching the ground,\nresulting in mainly sprinkles or isolated pockets of one to two\nhundredths of an inch of light rain across northeast Minnesota \nthrough late afternoon as temperatures warm to the mid 30s to \nnear 40 degrees. As surface temperatures drop this evening and \nthe band encounters slightly better low-level moisture over the \nMinnesota Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin, precipitation may \ntransition into a light mix of rain, freezing rain, and sleet. \nWhile widespread icing is unlikely, pockets of a light glaze of \nice could form on elevated and untreated surfaces toward \nmidnight tonight in the Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin before\nconditions dry out overnight.\n\nWinds will shift westerly late tonight and turn breezy on Saturday,\ngusting around 20 mph under partly sunny skies. A relatively mild\nair mass will linger across our region during the day Saturday, with\nhighs once again reaching the upper 30s to lower 40s. However, \nthe cold sector of a low pressure system passing to the north in\nOntario will finally push cold air advection into the Northland\nSaturday night. Winds will shift further to the northwest and \nincrease in speed overnight, bringing light snow showers mainly \nacross northern Minnesota. Some locations north of US Highway 2 \ncould wake up Sunday morning to a fresh coating of a few tenths \nof an inch of new snowfall.\n\nTruly colder air settles in to close out the holiday weekend on\nSunday as daytime temperatures only peak around 30 degrees. When\ncombined with northwest wind gusts of 20 to 25 mph, outdoor wind\nchills will feel like the mid to upper teens through the day. Off-\nand-on snow showers will persist across far northern Minnesota \non Sunday, aided by moisture from still-open large inland lakes.\nLocations downwind of Lake of the Woods and Rainy Lake may see \nlocalized snow shower enhancement, though total weekend \naccumulations across the region will remain under one inch.\n\nBy Monday, dry and quiet weather returns under a quasi-zonal upper-\nlevel flow pattern as winds ease and clouds gradually decrease.\nTemperatures remain chilly on Monday with highs in the upper 20s to\nlower 30s before a warming trend begins early next week. A weak\nshortwave disturbance moving through the Upper Midwest Monday night\ninto Tuesday will bring a 10-20 percent chance for light snow \nacross parts of the region.\n\nFrom Wednesday through Thursday, another weak disturbance will \nbring additional 10 to 25 percent chances for light rain and \nsnow showers. No major winter storm systems are signaled through\nThursday of next week, keeping our region in a largely low- \nimpact pattern. Temperatures will rebound once again to above \nnormal levels by Wednesday and Thursday, with afternoon highs \nclimbing back into the upper 30s and lower 40s across northeast \nMinnesota and northwest Wisconsin.",
